ABSTRACT

This thesis establishes the theoretical foundations for an agentic orchestration architecture that generates 3D narrative exploration games from natural language specifications. Its central object is a Game Intermediate Representation (Game IR) — an executable, engine-neutral specification formalized as a network of timed automata with explicit concurrency, urgency, Zeno-freedom, and observation semantics.

The thesis makes five bounded contributions. First, it formalizes the Game IR under a five-layer contract separating the typed world model, the executable transition model, requirements and contracts, the engine adapter, and runtime evidence. Second, it states a conditional finite-horizon trace correspondence theorem: if a matching relation between an IR and its compiled engine system satisfies explicit transfer, action-matching, clock-correspondence, and observation-bound conditions, then observable traces correspond up to a declared horizon. Third, it distinguishes four termination properties — existential winning reachability, universal recoverability, deadlock freedom, and bounded completion — and provides complexity results for each under explicitly specified input encodings. Fourth, it specifies a Godot 4 adapter architecture with declared error decomposition, horizon-bounded composition rules, and mandatory state resynchronization checkpoints. Fifth, it formulates a reachability-preserving pacing contract as an adapter-verified invariant and provides a symbolic backward-reachability governor.

The thesis deliberately avoids unconditional claims. The matching relation is not derived from capability declarations. Termination is not conflated with reachability. Engine independence is a design objective, not a demonstrated property. Formal verification via UPPAAL is scoped to bounded horizons because TCTL model checking is EXPTIME-complete. Empirical superiority remains an open, falsifiable question.

Keywords: procedural game generation, intermediate representation, timed automata, conditional bisimulation, termination verification, complexity, pacing, reachability preservation, UPPAAL.

