1. INTRODUCTION

1.1 Research Context

Procedural Content Generation has been transformed by large language models capable of generating executable 3D games from natural language. Systems such as AutoUE, UniGen, and MAGIC generate scenes, gameplay code, and transition scripts. However, these systems share three structural limitations.

Engine coupling. Each system targets a single engine. Generated content is not semantically portable without rewriting.

Local verification. Verification by keypoint injection validates local mechanics but does not establish global completion properties.

Uncontrolled pacing. Target durations are specifications, not guaranteed properties. No identified prior work measures global progression duration and adjusts content while preserving completion guarantees.

1.2 Research Question

Under what semantic and operational conditions can an executable, engine-neutral intermediate representation for 3D narrative exploration games support bounded behavioral conformance checking, detection of completion-dead-end states, and runtime pacing adjustments that preserve modeled winning reachability?

This formulation focuses on conditions under which specific properties hold, rather than asserting unconditional properties.

1.3 Hypotheses

H1 (Conditional Conformance). For a defined class of 3D narrative exploration games, a Game IR with explicit transition semantics and a verification loop improves functional conformance and detection of completion-dead-end states relative to direct engine-specific generation, under matched generation budgets.

H2 (Reachability-Preserving Pacing). Runtime pacing configurations satisfying a formally specified transition-preservation contract maintain model-level winning reachability.

H3 (Two-Level Pacing). Offline structural pacing and verified runtime modulation reduce deviation from a predefined completion-time distribution without violating declared safety and reachability properties.

H4 (Bounded Portability). For the subset of Game IR capabilities supported by two independently implemented adapters, observable behavior conforms to a common reference semantics within declared, empirically tested bounds.

1.4 Claim Status Discipline

Every mathematical statement is classified as established, conditional, preliminary, or unverified. No theorem is stated as unconditional unless its assumptions are fully discharged. The trace correspondence theorem is conditional. Termination complexity results are established for specified encodings. Pacing preservation is stated as a contract to be verified, not as a theorem derived from variable naming.

1.5 Structure

Section 2 formalizes the Game IR. Section 3 defines the condition language. Section 4 states the conditional trace correspondence theorem. Section 5 analyzes four termination properties. Section 6 establishes complexity results. Section 7 specifies the Godot 4 adapter. Section 8 develops the error composition framework. Section 9 addresses UPPAAL translation and its scope. Section 10 specifies the pacing contract. Section 11 presents the pacing governor. Section 12 describes empirical validation. Section 13 presents the collision methodology. Section 14 specifies the experimental protocol. Section 15 discusses threats to validity. Section 16 concludes.

