2. THE GAME IR AS A TIMED AUTOMATON NETWORK

2.1 Five-Layer Contract

The Game IR is organized under a five-layer contract. Layers L1 and L2 carry executable semantics; L3 carries requirements; L4 declares adapter capabilities; L5 records runtime evidence. Annotations in L1 that affect transitions must carry explicit semantics.

Layer Content Role
L1 · Typed World Model Entities, geometry, zones, assets, transforms Static structure
L2 · Executable Transition Model Locations, actions, guards, updates, clocks, invariants Operational semantics
L3 · Requirements and Contracts Safety, reachability, recoverability, observations, timing Verification targets
L4 · Engine Adapter Capability mapping, compilation, declared approximation Compilation interface
L5 · Runtime Evidence Traces, counterexamples, conformance tests, audit records Empirical grounding

2.2 Definition

Definition 2.1 (Game IR). A Game IR is a tuple:

\mathcal{I} = (\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{C}, \mathcal{V}, \mathcal{V}_S, \mathcal{V}_Q, s_0, \mathbb{C}, O, \Phi, \Psi)

where:

· $\mathcal{A} = \{A_1, \ldots, A_n\}$ is a finite set of timed automata;
· $\mathcal{C}$ is a finite set of clock variables;
· $\mathcal{V} = \mathcal{V}_S \uplus \mathcal{V}_Q$ is a finite set of discrete variables, partitioned into structural ($\mathcal{V}_S$) and QoS ($\mathcal{V}_Q$) variables;
· $s_0$ is the initial global state;
· $\mathbb{C}$ is a finite set of functional constraints;
· $O$ is a finite set of objectives;
· $\Phi$ is a finite set of safety and liveness properties;
· $\Psi$ is a finite set of pacing configurations.

Each timed automaton is:

A_i = (L_i, \ell_i^0, \Sigma_i, C_i, E_i, \mathrm{Inv}_i, \mathrm{Urg}_i)

where:

· $L_i$ is a finite set of locations;
· $\ell_i^0 \in L_i$ is the initial location;
· $\Sigma_i$ is a finite alphabet of actions;
· $C_i \subseteq \mathcal{C}$ is the set of clocks read or reset by $A_i$;
· $E_i \subseteq L_i \times \mathrm{Guards}(C_i, \mathcal{V}) \times \Sigma_i \times 2^{C_i} \times L_i$ is the set of edges;
· $\mathrm{Inv}_i : L_i \to \mathrm{Invariants}(C_i)$ assigns a downward-closed clock constraint to each location;
· $\mathrm{Urg}_i \subseteq L_i$ is the set of urgent locations.

2.3 Concurrency Semantics

Real engines execute simultaneous updates. We adopt a synchronous product semantics with an explicit interleaving fallback for actions declared as asynchronous.

Definition 2.2 (Global State). A global state is a tuple:

s = (\vec{\ell}, \nu, v)

where $\vec{\ell} = (\ell_1, \ldots, \ell_n)$ is the location vector, $\nu : \mathcal{C} \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ is a clock valuation, and $v : \mathcal{V} \to D$ is a discrete valuation. The invariant condition is $\nu \models \bigwedge_i \mathrm{Inv}_i(\ell_i)$.

Definition 2.3 (Synchronous Transition). A synchronous transition is:

(\vec{\ell}, \nu, v) \xrightarrow{A \subseteq \Sigma} (\vec{\ell}', \nu', v')

where for each $i \in \mathrm{src}(A)$, an edge $(\ell_i, g_i, a_i, r_i, \ell_i') \in E_i$ with $a_i \in A$ is enabled ($\nu \models g_i$), and for $i \notin \mathrm{src}(A)$, $\ell_i' = \ell_i$.

Definition 2.4 (Asynchronous Transition). An asynchronous transition fires exactly one automaton with a single enabled edge.

The adapter declares, per automaton, whether its actions are synchronous or asynchronous.

2.4 Urgency and Zeno-Freedom

Definition 2.5 (Urgent Location). A location $\ell_i \in \mathrm{Urg}_i$ forbids time elapse: any delay transition must have duration $d = 0$.

Definition 2.6 (Zeno Execution). A timed trace is Zeno if it contains infinitely many discrete transitions within a finite total delay.

Assumption 2.1 (Zeno-Freedom). All Game IR instances considered are Zeno-free. This is enforced by a static check: every cycle in the location graph must contain at least one clock that is reset and whose invariant bounds the cycle duration from below by a positive constant.

Without this assumption, a game model could permit infinitely many events in finite time, rendering duration measurement ill-defined.

2.5 Observation and Metric

Definition 2.7 (Observation Space). An observation is a tuple:

o = (\mathrm{pos}, \mathrm{inv}, \mathrm{evt}, \nu_{\mathrm{obs}}) \in \mathcal{O}

where $\mathrm{pos} : \mathcal{E} \to \mathbb{R}^3$ is the position map over a declared set of observed entities, $\mathrm{inv}$ is the inventory valuation, $\mathrm{evt}$ is the most recent observable event, and $\nu_{\mathrm{obs}}$ is the projection of the clock valuation onto $\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{obs}} \subseteq \mathcal{C}$.

Definition 2.8 (Observation Projection). $\pi : S \to \mathcal{O}$ maps a global state to its observation.

Definition 2.9 (Observation Metric). The observation metric is:

d_{\mathcal{O}}(o_1, o_2) = \alpha \cdot \max_{e \in \mathcal{E}} \| \mathrm{pos}_1(e) - \mathrm{pos}_2(e) \|_2 + \beta \cdot d_{\mathrm{inv}}(\mathrm{inv}_1, \mathrm{inv}_2) + \gamma \cdot d_{\mathrm{evt}}(\mathrm{evt}_1, \mathrm{evt}_2)

where $\alpha, \beta, \gamma > 0$ are declared weights. Missing entities are assigned a sentinel position at distance $D_{\max}$ from all present entities.

Proposition 2.1. Under the declared convention for missing entities and positive weights, $d_{\mathcal{O}}$ is a metric on $\mathcal{O}$.

Proof. Symmetry, non-negativity, and identity of indiscernibles follow from the constituent metrics. The triangle inequality follows from the triangle inequalities of $\|\cdot\|_2$, $d_{\mathrm{inv}}$, and $d_{\mathrm{evt}}$, combined with positive weights. $\square$

2.6 Adapter Capabilities

Definition 2.10 (Capability). A capability $\kappa$ is an engine feature required by the executable layers. The adapter declares $\kappa$ as one of: SUPPORTED with $\varepsilon_\kappa = 0$; APPROXIMATED with a declared bound $\varepsilon_\kappa > 0$; UNSUPPORTED.

Rule 2.1 (Compilation). If any required capability is UNSUPPORTED, compilation fails. If capabilities are APPROXIMATED, the adapter must provide an explicit error-composition rule $f : \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^m \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ mapping component error bounds to a global bound.

Limitation 2.1. The declared bound $\varepsilon_\kappa$ is an adapter assertion, not a verified fact. Its correctness is an empirical obligation.

