3. CONDITION LANGUAGE SEMANTICS

3.1 Syntax

Definition 3.1 (Language $\mathcal{L}$).

\varphi ::= \top \mid \bot \mid x = v \mid x < v \mid x > v \mid x \leq v \mid x \geq v


\mid \mathrm{near}(e_1, e_2, d) \mid \mathrm{inside}(e, z) \mid \mathrm{has\_item}(e, i)


\mid \mathrm{objective\_completed}(o) \mid \mathrm{variable\_value}(x, v)


\mid \mathrm{time\_since}(ev) \leq t \mid \mathrm{time\_since}(ev) \geq t


\mid \varphi \wedge \varphi \mid \varphi \vee \varphi \mid \neg \varphi


\mid \mathrm{before}(ev_1, ev_2) \mid \mathrm{after}(ev_1, ev_2) \mid \mathrm{during}(ev, t_1, t_2)

3.2 Denotational Semantics

Definition 3.2. Let $s = (\vec{\ell}, \nu, v)$. Then:

\llbracket x = v \rrbracket(s) = (v(x) = v)


\llbracket \mathrm{near}(e_1, e_2, d) \rrbracket(s) = (\mathrm{dist}(s(e_1), s(e_2)) \leq d)


\llbracket \mathrm{time\_since}(ev) \leq t \rrbracket(s) = (\nu(\mathrm{clock}_{ev}) \leq t)


\llbracket \varphi \wedge \psi \rrbracket(s) = \llbracket \varphi \rrbracket(s) \wedge \llbracket \psi \rrbracket(s)

Property 3.1. The semantics is compositional and, given $s$, assigns a unique truth value to every $\varphi$.

3.3 Typing

Theorem 3.1 (Typing Safety). If $\varphi$ is well-typed in $\Gamma$, then for all valid $s$, $\llbracket \varphi \rrbracket(s)$ is defined.

Theorem 3.2 (Decidability). Type checking for $\mathcal{L}$ is decidable in polynomial time.

Proof. The typing rules are syntax-directed. Each rule application is deterministic and bounded by the size of the abstract syntax tree. Standard unification completes in near-linear time. $\square$

