5. TERMINATION: FOUR DISTINCT PROPERTIES

5.1 Motivation

The single predicate $\mathrm{Reach}_F(s_0) = 1$ establishes only existential winning reachability. We distinguish four properties with distinct operational meanings.

Let $\mathcal{G} = (S_R, E)$ be the reachable state graph from $s_0$, and $F \subseteq S_R$ the set of terminal (victory) states.

Definition 5.1 (Winning Reachability).

\mathrm{WinReach}(\mathcal{G}) = 1 \iff \exists f \in F : s_0 \leadsto f

Definition 5.2 (Universal Recoverability).

\mathrm{UnivRec}(\mathcal{G}) = 1 \iff \forall s \in S_R, \ s \notin F \Rightarrow \exists f \in F : s \leadsto f

Definition 5.3 (Deadlock Freedom).

\mathrm{DeadlockFree}(\mathcal{G}) = 1 \iff \forall s \in S_R \setminus F : \mathrm{out}(s) \neq \emptyset

Definition 5.4 (Bounded Completion). Let $\rho$ be a player policy. Then:

\mathrm{BoundedComplete}(\mathcal{G}, \rho, N) = 1 \iff \text{under } \rho, \text{ a state in } F \text{ is reached within } N \text{ steps}

5.2 Completion Dead Ends

Definition 5.5 (Completion Dead End). A state $s \in S_R$ is a completion dead end if:

s \notin F \ \wedge \ \neg \exists f \in F : s \leadsto f

Theorem 5.1 (Characterization). A state $s \in S_R$ is a completion dead end if and only if $\mathrm{Reach}_F(s) = 0$.

Proof. Immediate from Definition 5.5. $\square$

Remark 5.1 (Terminology). A completion dead end need not be a graph-theoretic deadlock. It may have outgoing transitions indefinitely while never reaching victory. We reserve "deadlock" for states with $\mathrm{out}(s) = \emptyset$.

5.3 Local Verification Does Not Imply Global Termination

Proposition 5.1. Satisfaction of all tested local keypoints does not imply global winning reachability.

Proof. Let $k_1, \ldots, k_n$ be keypoints, each locally satisfiable. Construct a Game IR in which opening door A before lever B disables B permanently, and the victory condition requires B. Then each $k_i$ is satisfiable along some trace, but $\mathrm{WinReach}(\mathcal{G}) = 0$. $\square$

