6. COMPLEXITY OF VERIFICATION

6.1 Input Encodings

Complexity claims require specified encodings.

Definition 6.1 (Explicit Encoding). The graph $\mathcal{G} = (S_R, E)$ is given as an adjacency list. Input size is $|S_R| + |E|$.

Definition 6.2 (Implicit Encoding). The graph is represented by a tuple $(\mathcal{I}, \mathrm{enc})$ where $\mathrm{enc}$ is a polynomial-time computable successor function $\mathrm{succ} : S_R \to 2^{S_R}$. States and transitions are not enumerated. Input size is $|\mathcal{I}|$.

6.2 Established Complexity Results

Theorem 6.1 (Explicit Deadlock Detection). Given an explicit graph $\mathcal{G} = (S_R, E)$ and $F \subseteq S_R$, the decision problem "does there exist $s \in S_R$ with $\mathrm{Reach}_F(s) = 0$?" is in $\mathsf{P}$.

Proof. Compute $\mathrm{Reach}_F$ for all states by reverse reachability from $F$. Time: $O(|S_R| + |E|)$. $\square$

Theorem 6.2 (Implicit Winning Reachability). Given an implicit encoding $(\mathcal{I}, \mathrm{enc})$ with polynomial successor enumeration and polynomial-size state encodings, the decision problem $\mathrm{WinReach}(\mathcal{G}) = 1$ is in $\mathsf{PSPACE}$.

Proof. Non-deterministic reachability exploration in polynomial space suffices. By Savitch's theorem, $\mathsf{NPSPACE} = \mathsf{PSPACE}$. $\square$

6.3 Hardness — Scoped Claims

Proposition 6.1 (Conditional PSPACE-Hardness). If the implicit encoding permits a polynomial-time reduction from a $\mathsf{PSPACE}$-hard planning problem, then $\mathrm{WinReach}$ is $\mathsf{PSPACE}$-hard for that encoding.

Proof sketch. The reduction must construct, from an instance of the hard planning problem, a Game IR $\mathcal{I}$ whose successor function corresponds to the planning transition, and whose terminal states correspond to plan completion. $\square$

Remark 6.1. PSPACE-hardness does not follow merely from implicitness. It requires an explicit reduction. We do not claim $\mathsf{PSPACE}$-completeness for the general Game IR verification problem.

6.4 Timed and Strategic Extensions

For dense-time clocks, reachability is $\mathsf{PSPACE}$-complete under standard clock constraints (Alur–Dill). For TCTL model checking with leads-to operators on timed automata, the complexity is $\mathsf{EXPTIME}$-complete. For ATL and ATL* over propositional GDL, model checking is $\mathsf{EXPTIME}$-complete. These are different problems with different decision procedures and must not be conflated.

Remark 6.2 (Verification Scope). The $\mathsf{EXPTIME}$-completeness of TCTL model checking implies that exhaustive verification is only feasible for small games. For realistic sizes, bounded falsification is the appropriate methodology.

6.5 Bounded Falsification

Methodological Choice 6.1. For realistically sized games, exhaustive termination verification is algorithmically costly. We adopt bounded falsification: search for completion dead ends up to a depth $k$, returning either a counterexample or the declaration "no dead end found up to depth $k$."

