7. GODOT 4 L4 ADAPTER ARCHITECTURE

7.1 Compilation Pipeline

The L4 adapter is a formal compiler that converts the verified Game IR tuple $\mathcal{I}$ into Godot 4 scene trees (.tscn) and GDScript execution scripts. The pipeline proceeds through five stages:

1. AST ingestion and capability gatekeeping. Parse the Game IR and evaluate required capabilities against declared SUPPORTED/APPROXIMATED/UNSUPPORTED status.
2. Scene graph generation. L1 world model → Godot 4 .tscn tree.
3. State machine generation. L2 timed automata → GDScript node systems.
4. Clock integration. $\mathcal{C}$ → frame accumulator integrated with _physics_process(delta).
5. Error composition monitoring. $f(\varepsilon_1, \ldots, \varepsilon_m) \leq \varepsilon_{\mathrm{global}}$ enforced at compile time.

7.2 Structural Compilation Rules

Game IR Construct Godot 4 Mapping Runtime Semantics
World entity (L1) Node3D / RigidBody3D / Area3D Scene graph node
Automaton $A_i$ Node with TimedAutomatonNode script Maintains location $\ell_i$
Location $\ell_i$ GDScript enum state Enum constant
Clock $c$ float accumulator Incremented by delta in _physics_process
Urgent location Immediate tick evaluation Evaluated in-frame
Action $\sigma$ Godot signal SignalBus.emit_signal("action_name", payload)
Guard $g$ GDScript boolean function Evaluated during transition checks

7.3 Clock Discretization and Physics Tick Jitter

Godot executes physics at a fixed tick rate (default 60 Hz) with potential frame-rate jitter. The adapter tracks discretized clock evaluation at frame $k$:

c(t_k) = \sum_{j=1}^{k} \Delta t_j

Frame-rate jitter introduces drift bounded by:

\varepsilon_{\mathrm{clk}}(K) = \sup_k \left| \sum_{j=1}^{k} \Delta t_j - k \cdot \Delta t_{\mathrm{target}} \right| \leq K \cdot \delta_{\mathrm{jitter}}

Critical Note. This bound is not guaranteed by Godot; actual jitter depends on OS scheduling and max_physics_steps_per_frame. The adapter must either enforce a hard bound via Engine.max_physics_steps_per_frame or measure delta_jitter empirically and declare it as an APPROXIMATED capability bound.

7.4 State Resynchronization Checkpoints

Definition 7.1 (Resynchronization Checkpoint). A resynchronization checkpoint is a frame index $k^*$ at which the engine state $\pi_e(s^e_{k^*})$ is compared to the IR observation $\pi(s_{k^*})$. If $d_{\mathcal{O}}(\pi(s_{k^*}), \pi_e(s^e_{k^*})) > \varepsilon_{\mathrm{global}}$, the engine state is corrected to match the IR state.

Design Requirement. Without resynchronization checkpoints, the theoretical error bound $L^K e_0 + \eta (L^K - 1)/(L - 1)$ grows exponentially with horizon and becomes vacuous. Resynchronization is therefore a necessary design element, not an optional optimization.

