8. ERROR COMPOSITION FRAMEWORK

8.1 Error Source Decomposition

The vector of component errors $\vec{\varepsilon} = (\varepsilon_{\mathrm{clk}}, \varepsilon_{\mathrm{kin}}, \varepsilon_{\mathrm{pen}}, \varepsilon_{\mathrm{fp}})$ captures four engine artifacts during physics ticks.

8.2 Clock Discretization Error

\varepsilon_{\mathrm{clk}}(K) \leq K \cdot \delta_{\mathrm{jitter}}

where $\delta_{\mathrm{jitter}}$ is the empirically measured or enforced maximum jitter per frame.

8.3 Kinematic Integration Error

Godot uses semi-implicit (symplectic) Euler integration for RigidBody3D and position-based motion for CharacterBody3D. The per-step position error for symplectic Euler is first order in $\Delta t$:

e_{\mathrm{kin},k} \leq C \cdot \|v_k\|_2 \cdot \Delta t_k + C' \cdot \|a_k\|_2 \cdot \Delta t_k^2

for constants $C, C'$ depending on the specific integrator. The adapter declares which integration scheme is used per entity type.

8.4 Physics Solver Penetration

Jolt Physics uses Baumgarte stabilization with default factor $\mu_{\mathrm{solver}} = 0.2$. Since stabilization reduces penetration over time, the bound is max-based, not sum-based:

\varepsilon_{\mathrm{pen}}(K) \leq \max_k \left( \mu_{\mathrm{solver}} \cdot n_{\mathrm{contacts},k} \cdot \delta_{\mathrm{pen}} \right) \cdot \left( 1 + \frac{1}{1 - \mu_{\mathrm{solver}}} \right)

8.5 Floating-Point Error

For single-precision IEEE 754 (float32), machine epsilon $u = 2^{-24} \approx 5.96 \times 10^{-8}$:

\varepsilon_{\mathrm{fp}}(K) = K \cdot u \cdot \max_{e \in \mathcal{E}} \|\mathbf{x}_e\|_\infty

The adapter declares precision mode (single or double) and adjusts $u$.

8.6 Error Coupling Recurrence

Let $\mathbf{e}_k = \mathbf{x}_k^{\mathrm{engine}} - \mathbf{x}_k^{\mathrm{IR}}$ be the spatial error at step $k$. For a transition map Lipschitz in spatial coordinates with constant $L_x$ and in velocity with constant $L_v$:

\|\mathbf{e}_{k+1}\|_2 \leq (1 + L_x \Delta t) \|\mathbf{e}_k\|_2 + L_v \Delta t \|\mathbf{v}_k^{\mathrm{err}}\|_2 + \eta_k

where $\eta_k$ aggregates kinematic, penetration, and floating-point perturbations.

With $L = 1 + L_x \Delta t_{\max}$, the closed-form bound over $K$ steps is:

\|\mathbf{e}_K\|_2 \leq L^K \|\mathbf{e}_0\|_2 + \sum_{k=0}^{K-1} L^k \eta_{K-1-k}

Remark 8.1. As established in Section 4.4, for $L > 1$, this bound grows exponentially with $K$ and becomes vacuous for long horizons. Resynchronization checkpoints (Definition 7.1) reset $\|\mathbf{e}_0\|$ periodically and restore a meaningful bound.

8.7 Composition Operators

A. Worst-Case Deterministic Bound ($f_{\mathrm{det}}$). When strict safety verification is required:

f_{\mathrm{det}}(\vec{\varepsilon}; K) = \alpha \left[ L^K \|\mathbf{e}_0\|_2 + \frac{L^K - 1}{L - 1} \eta_{\max} \right] + \gamma \cdot \mathbb{I}(\tau_{\mathrm{guard}} > \Delta t_{\max})

where $\eta_{\max}$ is the worst-case per-step disturbance and $\mathbb{I}(\cdot)$ is the indicator of guard-frame misalignment.

B. Root-Sum-Square Stochastic Bound ($f_{\mathrm{rss}}$). Under uncorrelated zero-mean disturbances:

f_{\mathrm{rss}}(\vec{\varepsilon}; K) = \alpha \sqrt{K \left( \|v_{\max}\| \sigma_{\mathrm{jitter}} \right)^2 + K \left( \frac{1}{2} a_{\max} \Delta t_{\max}^2 \right)^2 + K \sigma_{\mathrm{pen}}^2 + K \sigma_{\mathrm{fp}}^2}

The $\sqrt{K}$ growth is favorable relative to the exponential $L^K$ growth.

8.8 Compilation Gate

During compilation, the L4 adapter evaluates:

\text{CompileStatus} = \begin{cases} \mathbf{SUCCESS}(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{declared}} = f_{\mathrm{rss}}), & \text{if } f_{\mathrm{rss}} \leq \varepsilon_{\mathrm{global}} \\ \mathbf{REMEDIATE}(\text{resync checkpoints}), & \text{if } f_{\mathrm{det}} > \varepsilon_{\mathrm{global}} \text{ but resync feasible} \\ \mathbf{FAIL}(\kappa \gets \mathrm{UNSUPPORTED}), & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

Note. The primary gate uses $f_{\mathrm{rss}}$, not $f_{\mathrm{det}}$, because $f_{\mathrm{det}}$ is vacuous for long horizons without resynchronization. Resynchronization checkpoints are declared as a capability parameter with their frequency $\nu_{\mathrm{resync}}$.

