9. UPPAAL TRANSLATION AND VERIFICATION SCOPE

9.1 AST Mapping

The Game IR is mapped to UPPAAL XML (.xml) and L3 properties to TCTL queries (.q).

· Clocks. Every $c \in \mathcal{C}$ is declared as clock c;.
· Variables. Discrete $x \in \mathcal{V}$ become int[min, max] x; or bool x;.
· Urgent locations. Rendered with <urgent/>.
· Synchronous actions. Mapped to chan a; with a! (emit) and a? (receive). Broadcast channels model 1-to-N triggers.

9.2 TCTL Query Mapping

Property TCTL Query
Winning Reachability E<> (GameManager.Victory)
Universal Recoverability GameManager.NotTerminal --> GameManager.Victory
Deadlock Freedom A[] (not GameManager.Victory imply not deadlock)
Bounded Completion E<> (GameManager.Victory and global_clock <= N)

9.3 Expressiveness and Complexity Scope

Critical Scope. The complexity of TCTL model checking over timed automata is $\mathsf{EXPTIME}$-complete. Restricted fragments (simple reachability) can be $\mathsf{PSPACE}$-complete, but the full TCTL fragment including the leads-to operator is $\mathsf{EXPTIME}$-complete.

For a network with $n$ timed automata and $|\mathcal{C}|$ clocks, the UPPAAL state space is the product of all automaton locations times the region graph of clock valuations. The number of regions is:

O\left(|\mathcal{C}|! \cdot \prod_{c \in \mathcal{C}} (2 \cdot \max_c)\right)

which grows factorially with $|\mathcal{C}|$.

Implication. Exhaustive UPPAAL verification is feasible only for small games with a small number of clocks. For realistic sizes, the adapter must use one of:

1. Bounded horizon verification via UPPAAL's --search-order with depth limits;
2. Statistical model checking (UPPAAL SMC) rather than exhaustive verification;
3. Clock restriction with $|\mathcal{C}| \leq 3$ and APPROXIMATED declaration for larger games.

Methodological Choice 9.1. The adapter's default verification mode is bounded falsification with horizon $K_{\mathrm{UPPAAL}}$, not exhaustive verification. Exhaustive verification is reserved for small verification targets (individual mechanics, small subgames).

