10. REACHABILITY-PRESERVING PACING CONTRACT

10.1 Two-Level Pacing

Definition 10.1 (Structural Pacing). Structural pacing operates offline, before verification. It modifies the automaton network (locations, structural guards) and triggers a full termination verification pass.

Definition 10.2 (Dynamic Pacing). Dynamic pacing operates online, during execution. It modifies only variables in $\mathcal{V}_Q$, subject to the contract below.

10.2 The Contract

Definition 10.3 (Pacing Configuration). A pacing configuration is a valuation $q \in Q$ of the QoS variables $\mathcal{V}_Q$. Applying $q$ yields a runtime system $M_q$ with the same state space $S$, the same terminal states $F$, and possibly a modified transition relation $T_q$.

Definition 10.4 (Safe Configuration Set). The set $Q_{\mathrm{safe}} \subseteq Q$ is the largest set satisfying:

\forall q \in Q_{\mathrm{safe}}, \quad \mathrm{Reach}_F^{M_q}(s_0) = \mathrm{Reach}_F^{M}(s_0)

10.3 Contract Obligation

Proposition 10.1 (Contract Obligation). The contract in Definition 10.4 is an adapter obligation. It cannot be derived from the naming of variables in $\mathcal{V}_Q$.

Justification. A variable named "hint density" can affect action availability, collision timing, resource consumption, or timeouts. If any such effect changes reachability to $F$, the contract is violated regardless of the variable's name.

Method for Establishing the Contract. The adapter must provide, for each $q \in Q_{\mathrm{safe}}$:

1. A structural abstraction $\alpha_q : S \to S_\alpha$ such that $\alpha_q(s_0)$ and $\alpha_q(F)$ are identical across all $q \in Q_{\mathrm{safe}}$;
2. A proof that the abstract transition relation is preserved;
3. A refinement proof that the abstraction preserves reachability to $F$.

Alternatively, the adapter may exhaustively verify the contract on a bounded state space.

10.4 Decidability Caveat

For dense-time systems, exact $\mathrm{Pre}^*$ computation is not decidable. The adapter must use one of:

1. Finite abstractions (region graphs) to compute an over-approximation of $\mathrm{Pre}^*$;
2. Bounded $\mathrm{Pre}^*$ computation with depth limits;
3. Declaration of $Q_{\mathrm{safe}}$ as an APPROXIMATED contract with bounded horizon.

10.5 Outcome Metrics

We replace the single "agency loss" scalar with a declared outcome vector:

\mathrm{Outcome}(q) = (\Delta h, \Delta o, \Delta p, \Delta t, \Delta a)

where $\Delta h$: change in hints per objective; $\Delta o$: change in interaction opportunities; $\Delta p$: change in completion probability; $\Delta t$: change in completion time; $\Delta a$: subjective perceived autonomy.

