14. EXPERIMENTAL PROTOCOL

14.1 Experimental Conditions

Condition Description
B1 Direct engine-specific generation from prompt, no IR
B2 Game IR generation, compilation, no verification loop
B3 Game IR + local keypoint verification
B4 Game IR + local verification + bounded termination verification + pacing agent
B5 Game IR + local verification + bounded termination verification, pacing disabled

B4 vs. B5 estimates the incremental effect of the pacing system with the remaining pipeline held constant.

14.2 Metric Hierarchy

Each metric establishes a distinct property. They are not substitutes.

Metric What It Establishes
Strict launch success Project starts without specified failures
Local requirement fidelity Tested keypoints pass their defined checks
Bounded test-agent completion Designated agent reaches victory within budget
Model-level winning reachability A path to victory exists in the formal model
Model-level recoverability Relevant reachable states retain a path to victory
Runtime-model agreement Engine transitions conform to the IR
Human playability Participants can complete the experience
Human pacing Observed duration under human play

14.3 Primary Endpoint and Estimand

Primary endpoint: Bounded test-agent completion (binary).

Estimand: Prompt-level paired difference:

\Delta_{c, c'} = \mathbb{E}_p \left[ \mathbb{E}_{r \mid p} \left[ Y_c(p, r) - Y_{c'}(p, r) \right] \right]

Estimated by:

\widehat{\Delta}_{c, c'} = \frac{1}{|P|} \sum_{p \in P} \left( \frac{1}{R} \sum_{r=1}^{R} \left[ Y_c(p, r) - Y_{c'}(p, r) \right] \right)

14.4 Paired Analysis

Repetitions from the same prompt are not independent. We use paired differences at the prompt level, hierarchical or cluster-robust standard errors, and matched seeds across conditions where technically feasible.

14.5 Confidence Intervals and Corrections

Wilson intervals are used for individual binomial proportions. For paired differences, bootstrap confidence intervals clustered at the prompt level are reported. For multiple secondary metrics, Holm–Bonferroni (family-wise error) and Benjamini–Hochberg (false discovery rate) are applied. The primary hypothesis test is reserved for one predeclared endpoint.

14.6 Power Analysis

The repetition count $R$ is justified by a precision analysis. Given a plausible effect size $\delta$, a target half-width, and estimated between-prompt variance $\sigma^2_p$ and within-prompt variance $\sigma^2_w$:

|P| \geq \frac{z_{1-\alpha/2}^2 (\sigma^2_p + \sigma^2_w / R)}{(\text{half-width})^2}

Values of $\sigma^2_p$ and $\sigma^2_w$ are estimated from a pilot experiment.

14.7 Falsification Criteria

Hypothesis Falsification Condition
H1 $\Delta_{B_4, B_1} \leq 0$ on the primary endpoint
H2 Existence of $q \in Q_{\mathrm{safe}}$ with $\mathrm{Reach}_F^{M_q}(s_0) \neq \mathrm{Reach}_F^{M}(s_0)$
H3 No reduction in completion-time deviation under B4 vs. B5
H4 Empirical bound $\hat{\varepsilon}$ exceeds declared $\varepsilon$ for any capability

A negative result for H1 does not invalidate the thesis. It remains valid as formalization and negative empirical result.

14.8 Preregistration

The protocol is pre-registered on OSF before data collection. The preregistration includes hypotheses, conditions, metrics, sample sizes, statistical tests, and falsification criteria. Amendments are logged with timestamps.

