15. THREATS TO VALIDITY

15.1 Internal Validity

Confounding. B1 vs. B4 changes representation, verification, termination, and pacing simultaneously. B5 isolates pacing but not other components. A factorial design would isolate more effects but increases run count and implementation burden.

Stochasticity. LLM sampling variance is addressed by fixed temperature, declared seeds, and reporting across multiple seeds.

Contamination. Prompt novelty is tested against public corpora to reduce training-data leakage.

15.2 External Validity

Genre restriction. The evaluation targets 3D narrative exploration. Results may not generalize to other genres.

Engine restriction. The prototype uses Godot. Multi-engine portability is hypothesized (H4) but not demonstrated. A second adapter is required to test H4.

Prompt diversity. The prompt set is finite. Larger sets would improve coverage.

15.3 Construct Validity

Terminability. Bounded test-agent completion is not human completion. Both are reported separately.

Prompt fidelity. Keypoint validation measures explicit conformance, not subjective quality.

Duration. Automated test duration may differ from human duration. A human pilot calibrates the duration model.

15.4 Conclusion Validity

Multiple testing. Holm–Bonferroni and Benjamini–Hochberg correct for multiple comparisons under specified assumptions; the actual correlation structure among metrics is unknown.

Power. The pilot determines the required sample size; underpowered analyses report effect sizes without strong significance claims.

Negative results. Pre-registered. A negative result for H1 is a valid thesis outcome.

15.5 Model Fidelity

Abstract vs. concrete. Detection of completion dead ends in the abstract model does not capture practical unplayability (impossible jumps, non-intuitive paths, difficulty).

Adapter declaration trust. Declared capability bounds and pacing contract verifications are adapter assertions. Empirical validation is mandatory.

15.6 Horizon Limitation

Bounded correspondence. The trace correspondence theorem is finite-horizon. Infinite-horizon correspondence is not claimed and would require $L \leq 1$ or infinite resynchronization frequency.

