16. CONCLUSION

16.1 Summary of Contributions

Contribution 1 — Formal Game IR. A five-layer contract with executable semantics as a timed automaton network, explicit concurrency, urgency, Zeno-freedom, and observation metric.

Contribution 2 — Conditional Correspondence Theorem. A finite-horizon trace correspondence theorem whose hypotheses are explicit transfer, action-matching, clock-correspondence, and observation-bound conditions. The matching relation is not derived from capability declarations; it is assumed and must be established or checked.

Contribution 3 — Termination Analysis. Four distinct properties — winning reachability, universal recoverability, deadlock freedom, bounded completion — with complexity results for each under specified encodings. PSPACE-completeness is not claimed for the general verification problem; it is claimed only for encodings for which reductions are constructed. TCTL model checking is EXPTIME-complete, scoping verification to bounded horizons.

Contribution 4 — Godot 4 Adapter Architecture. An error composition framework with declared sources, semi-implicit Euler bounds, contractive penetration bounds, horizon-limited growth, and mandatory state resynchronization checkpoints. The compilation gate uses the RSS bound as primary, with the deterministic bound reported for reference.

Contribution 5 — Reachability-Preserving Pacing Contract. A formal invariant for dynamic pacing established by adapter contract, with a symbolic backward-reachability governor using bounded depth to preserve safety under decidability constraints.

Contribution 6 — Scoped Novelty and Pre-Registered Protocol. An auditable collision methodology and a pre-registered experimental protocol with paired estimands, hierarchical error accounting, and explicit falsification criteria.

16.2 Answer to the Research Question

The research question was:

Under what semantic and operational conditions can an executable, engine-neutral intermediate representation for 3D narrative exploration games support bounded behavioral conformance checking, detection of completion-dead-end states, and runtime pacing adjustments that preserve modeled winning reachability?

The answer is: under the conditions specified in Sections 2–11, namely:

· A Game IR formalized as a timed automaton network with explicit concurrency, urgency, and Zeno-freedom;
· An adapter that declares capabilities, provides an error-composition rule, and enforces resynchronization checkpoints;
· A matching relation satisfying the four transfer conditions of Theorem 4.1;
· An observation metric with declared weights and missing-entity conventions;
· A pacing contract established by adapter obligation, not variable naming, with bounded backward-reachability verification.

Under these conditions, the Game IR supports the three capabilities. Whether it improves generation outcomes relative to direct engine-specific methods remains an empirical question addressed by the protocol of Section 14.

16.3 Limitations

Limitation 1 — Model fidelity. Detection of completion dead ends in the abstract model does not capture practical unplayability.

Limitation 2 — Adapter declaration trust. Declared capability bounds and pacing contract verifications are adapter assertions. Empirical validation is mandatory.

Limitation 3 — Bounded horizon. The correspondence theorem is finite-horizon. Infinite-horizon correspondence is not claimed.

Limitation 4 — Engine restriction. The prototype is Godot-only. Multi-engine portability (H4) requires a second adapter to test.

Limitation 5 — Complexity scope. PSPACE-hardness is claimed only for encodings for which reductions are constructed. TCTL verification is EXPTIME-complete, scoping UPPAAL use to bounded horizons.

Limitation 6 — Undecidability of dense-time Pre*. Exact $\mathrm{Pre}^*$ is undecidable for dense-time systems. Pacing safety uses bounded or abstracted backward reachability.

16.4 Perspectives

Perspective 1 — Second adapter. Implementation of a second adapter (Unity or Unreal) would enable direct testing of H4.

Perspective 2 — Advanced model checking. Integration with bounded model checking for timed automata beyond UPPAAL's default, or with statistical model checking for larger horizons.

Perspective 3 — Learning-based pacing. Reinforcement learning of pacing configurations subject to the reachability-preservation contract, with the contract enforced as a hard constraint.

Perspective 4 — Human studies. Systematic evaluation of playability, pacing, and perceived autonomy beyond automated metrics.

16.5 Final Statement

This thesis establishes a theoretical foundation for treating generated games as executable specifications with explicit semantics, testable completion properties, and controlled runtime adaptation. It formalizes the Game IR under a five-layer contract, states a conditional finite-horizon trace correspondence theorem, distinguishes four termination properties with scoped complexity results, specifies a Godot 4 adapter with declared error decomposition and mandatory resynchronization, and formulates a reachability-preserving pacing contract with bounded symbolic verification. The question of empirical superiority remains open, but it is now posed in a falsifiable manner with pre-registered criteria.

The thesis deliberately does not claim what its proofs do not establish. The matching relation is not derived from capability declarations. Termination is not conflated with reachability. Engine independence is a design objective, not a demonstrated property. Exhaustive verification is scoped to bounded horizons where TCTL complexity permits. This discipline is the foundation on which subsequent empirical work can be honestly built.

