B.1 Proof of Proposition 2.1 (Observation Metric)

Proposition 2.1. Under the declared convention for missing entities and positive weights, $d_{\mathcal{O}}$ is a metric on $\mathcal{O}$.

Proof. We verify the three metric axioms.

(M1) Identity of indiscernibles. If $o_1 = o_2$, then $\mathrm{pos}_1(e) = \mathrm{pos}_2(e)$ for all $e \in \mathcal{E}$, $\mathrm{inv}_1 = \mathrm{inv}_2$, and $\mathrm{evt}_1 = \mathrm{evt}_2$. Hence all three terms vanish, so $d_{\mathcal{O}}(o_1, o_2) = 0$.

Conversely, suppose $d_{\mathcal{O}}(o_1, o_2) = 0$. Since $\alpha, \beta, \gamma > 0$ and all terms are non-negative, all three terms must be zero. Thus $\max_e \|\mathrm{pos}_1(e) - \mathrm{pos}_2(e)\|_2 = 0$, which implies $\mathrm{pos}_1 = \mathrm{pos}_2$ on $\mathcal{E}$. Similarly $d_{\mathrm{inv}}(\mathrm{inv}_1, \mathrm{inv}_2) = 0$ implies $\mathrm{inv}_1 = \mathrm{inv}_2$, and $d_{\mathrm{evt}}(\mathrm{evt}_1, \mathrm{evt}_2) = 0$ implies $\mathrm{evt}_1 = \mathrm{evt}_2$. Therefore $o_1 = o_2$.

(M2) Symmetry. Each constituent is symmetric: $\|\mathrm{pos}_1(e) - \mathrm{pos}_2(e)\|_2 = \|\mathrm{pos}_2(e) - \mathrm{pos}_1(e)\|_2$, $d_{\mathrm{inv}}$ is Hamming distance (symmetric), and $d_{\mathrm{evt}}$ is the discrete metric (symmetric). Positive-weighted sum of symmetric functions is symmetric.

(M3) Triangle inequality. Let $o_1, o_2, o_3 \in \mathcal{O}$. We show $d_{\mathcal{O}}(o_1, o_3) \leq d_{\mathcal{O}}(o_1, o_2) + d_{\mathcal{O}}(o_2, o_3)$.

For the spatial term:
\max_e \|\mathrm{pos}_1(e) - \mathrm{pos}_3(e)\|_2 \leq \max_e \left[ \|\mathrm{pos}_1(e) - \mathrm{pos}_2(e)\|_2 + \|\mathrm{pos}_2(e) - \mathrm{pos}_3(e)\|_2 \right]


\leq \max_e \|\mathrm{pos}_1(e) - \mathrm{pos}_2(e)\|_2 + \max_e \|\mathrm{pos}_2(e) - \mathrm{pos}_3(e)\|_2

For the inventory term, Hamming distance satisfies the triangle inequality on any finite-dimensional vector space.

For the event term, the discrete metric on any set satisfies the triangle inequality (it is a metric).

A positive-weighted sum of metrics is a metric. $\square$

B.2 Zeno-Freedom Sufficient Condition

Proposition B.1. A Game IR satisfies Assumption A.1 if there exists a ranking function $r : \mathcal{A} \times L \to \mathbb{N}$ such that for every cycle $\ell_1 \to \ell_2 \to \cdots \to \ell_k \to \ell_1$ in the location graph of any automaton $A_i$, there exists an edge $(\ell_j, g, a, r_{\mathrm{clk}}, \ell_{j+1})$ with $c \in r_{\mathrm{clk}}$ and $\mathrm{Inv}(\ell_{j+1}) \models c \geq \delta_{\min}$ for some $\delta_{\min} > 0$.

Proof. Consider a hypothetical Zeno execution. Any cycle traversal requires the designated edge to fire, which resets $c$ to $0$. To return to the cycle, the automaton must spend at least $\delta_{\min}$ time units in $\ell_{j+1}$ (by the invariant). Hence each cycle traversal consumes at least $\delta_{\min}$ time. Infinitely many traversals would require infinite time. Contradiction with Zeno definition. $\square$

B.3 State Space Finiteness

Proposition B.2. If the discrete variable domains are finite and the clock invariants are bounded, then the region-equivalence quotient of $S$ is finite.

Proof. Each clock $c \in \mathcal{C}$ has a maximum value $M_c = \max_{\ell \in L} \mathrm{Inv}(\ell)(c)$ (finite by assumption). The region equivalence partitions $\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{|\mathcal{C}|}$ into finitely many regions. The product with finitely many discrete valuations and location vectors is finite. $\square$

