APPENDIX C — PROOFS FOR CHAPTER 3 (CONDITION LANGUAGE)

C.1 Proof of Theorem 3.1 (Typing Safety)

Theorem 3.1. If $\varphi$ is well-typed in $\Gamma$, then for all valid $s$, $\llbracket \varphi \rrbracket(s)$ is defined.

Proof. By structural induction on $\varphi$.

Base cases.

· $\varphi = \top$ or $\bot$: $\llbracket \top \rrbracket(s) = \top$ is defined.
· $\varphi = (x = v)$: Well-typedness requires $x \in \Gamma$ and $v \in \mathrm{Type}(x)$. Since $s$ is valid, $v(x)$ is defined, so $\llbracket x = v \rrbracket(s) = (v(x) = v)$ is a defined boolean.
· $\varphi = \mathrm{near}(e_1, e_2, d)$: Well-typedness requires $e_1, e_2 \in \Gamma$ as entities and $d \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$. Since $s$ is valid, $s(e_1), s(e_2)$ are defined positions. Euclidean distance is computable. Defined.
· $\varphi = \mathrm{time\_since}(ev) \leq t$: Well-typedness requires $ev \in \Gamma$ as an event and $t \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$. Since $s$ is valid, $\nu(\mathrm{clock}_{ev})$ is defined. Defined.

Inductive cases.

· $\varphi = \psi_1 \wedge \psi_2$: By IH, $\llbracket \psi_1 \rrbracket(s)$ and $\llbracket \psi_2 \rrbracket(s)$ are defined booleans. Their conjunction is defined.
· $\varphi = \neg \psi$: By IH, $\llbracket \psi \rrbracket(s)$ is a defined boolean. Negation is defined.
· $\varphi = \mathrm{before}(ev_1, ev_2)$: Well-typedness requires $ev_1, ev_2 \in \Gamma$. Since $s$ is valid, the clock values $\mathrm{clock}_{ev_1}, \mathrm{clock}_{ev_2}$ are defined. The comparison $\mathrm{time}(ev_1) < \mathrm{time}(ev_2)$ is defined.

All cases preserve definedness. $\square$

C.2 Proof of Theorem 3.2 (Type Checking in PTIME)

Theorem 3.2. Type checking for $\mathcal{L}$ is decidable in polynomial time.

Proof. We construct a syntax-directed type-checking algorithm.

Algorithm.

1. Traverse the AST bottom-up.
2. For each node, apply the corresponding typing rule.
3. Look up identifiers in $\Gamma$ (constant time with hash table).
4. Unify types for polymorphic constructs.

Complexity analysis.

· AST traversal: $O(|\varphi|)$ nodes.
· Each node performs a constant number of $\Gamma$ lookups: $O(1)$ each.
· Unification of simple first-order types: linear in the size of the type expression.
· Total: $O(|\varphi| \cdot t_{\max})$ where $t_{\max}$ is the maximum type size, bounded by $|\Gamma|$.

Hence type checking is in PTIME. $\square$

C.3 Compositionality

Proposition C.1. For every $\varphi = \psi_1 \circ \psi_2$ with $\circ \in \{\wedge, \vee\}$, $\llbracket \varphi \rrbracket(s)$ depends only on $\llbracket \psi_1 \rrbracket(s)$ and $\llbracket \psi_2 \rrbracket(s)$.

Proof. By Definition 3.2, the semantics of $\varphi$ is defined by composition of the semantics of the subformulas. No other state components are consulted. $\square$

C.4 Determinism

Proposition C.2. For every $s \in S$ and $\varphi \in \mathcal{L}$, $\llbracket \varphi \rrbracket(s)$ is uniquely determined.

Proof. By induction on $\varphi$. The clock valuation $\nu$ is a function, so $\nu(\mathrm{clock}_{ev})$ is uniquely defined. Discrete valuations are functions. Distance computations are deterministic. Boolean operations are deterministic. $\square$

